CRITERION 3 — computeDashboardSync loads defs+pins, applies D-SY-e ordering,
NewMonthlyExpenses formula unchanged, ExcludedGroups per D-SY-d/e sorted by
Name.  RESULT: FAIL (one sub-clause of D-SY-d is measurably violated;
                     everything else in this criterion is PASS)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
PASSING PARTS:

(a) Loads via loader.LoadMajorExpenses()/loader.LoadTransactionPins() and
  classifies over the FILTERED outflows. Read at sync.go:149-174:
    filtered := data.Active().FilterByDateRange(yearAgo, now)
    outflows := filtered.FilterByType(models.Outflow)
    ...
    exclusions := majorexpenses.ComputePlanSyncExclusions(outflows, defs, pins)
  `outflows` is the 12-month-filtered set, as the criterion requires. The
  `months` block (sync.go:152-158) is byte-identical to master's.

(b) D-SY-e ordering (HI check FIRST) — PROVEN by mutation.
  Mutation M2: move the Health-Insurance `continue` to AFTER the exclusions
  lookup, i.e. exactly the ordering D-SY-e forbids.
  $ go test -overlay=<M2> -count=1 -run 'TestComputeDashboardSync_Excludes|TestHandleWhatIfSync_RendersExcludedGroupsSection' ./internal/handlers/whatif/
    --- FAIL: TestComputeDashboardSync_ExcludesFlaggedMajorExpenseGroup (0.02s)
        sync_plan_exclusions_test.go:142: excluded group count = 5, want 4 (the HI-overlap row must NOT be counted here)
        sync_plan_exclusions_test.go:146: excluded group total = 2500.00, want 2000.00
        sync_plan_exclusions_test.go:150: excluded group monthly = 246.58, want ~197.37
    --- FAIL: TestHandleWhatIfSync_RendersExcludedGroupsSection (0.01s)
        sync_plan_exclusions_test.go:221: preview missing excluded group count
  The 2500-vs-2000 delta is precisely the HI-overlap row (-500) leaking into
  the group total. D-SY-e's "excluded once, as HI, and NOT in the group total"
  is enforced by a test that dies when the ordering is swapped.

(c) NewMonthlyExpenses = |signed sum of remaining| / months, verified through
  the SAVED value (no rounding path) by the oracle:
    CHECK apply-saved-living: PASS
  The oracle asserts saved == 9500/months and its failure message names the
  two wrong answers it discriminates against (master ~11500/months, and
  flagged-def-filtering ~9000/months). Independently, the worker's
  TestComputeDashboardSync_ExcludesFlaggedMajorExpenseGroup asserts
  10500/months on its own fixture (10 groceries + the gym row that first-def-
  wins keeps in living).

FAILING PART — D-SY-d's determinism MUST:

D-SY-d states, normatively: "sorted by Name (determinism: syncPlanHash hashes
canonical JSON; the struct must stay map-free and slice order must be
deterministic or preview/apply hash equality breaks)."

`syncPlan` IS map-free (verified: syncPlanHash's own comment at sync.go:344
and the struct definition). `ExcludedGroups` IS sorted by Name (sync.go:218).
But the sort key is NOT UNIQUE and `sort.Slice` is NOT STABLE, so when two
flagged defs share a Name the slice order falls back to Go's randomized map
iteration over `excludedByID` (which is keyed by def.ID, not Name).

PROVEN EMPIRICALLY, not argued. Probe: two flagged defs both named "Car Loan"
(ids a/b, exact-amounts 500 and 700), 40 consecutive computeDashboardSync
calls against UNCHANGED data:
  $ go test -overlay=<probe> -count=1 -v -run TestProbeDuplicateNameDeterminism ./internal/handlers/whatif/
    PROBE-A distinct syncPlanHash values over 40 runs: 2
    PROBE-A ExcludedGroups order seen 36/40: [{"Name":"Car Loan","MonthlyAmount":49.31506849315068,"Total":500,"Count":1},{"Name":"Car Loan","MonthlyAmount":69.04109589041096,"Total":700,"Count":1}]
    PROBE-A ExcludedGroups order seen  4/40: [{"Name":"Car Loan","MonthlyAmount":69.04109589041096,"Total":700,"Count":1},{"Name":"Car Loan","MonthlyAmount":49.31506849315068,"Total":500,"Count":1}]
The two hashes are attributable to ExcludedGroups ORDER specifically — the
logged JSON shows the same two elements in swapped order and nothing else
differs.

USER-FACING CONSEQUENCE is the exact one D-SY-d names. sync.go:525-539:
    plan, err := computeDashboardSync(settings)
    hash, err := syncPlanHash(plan)
    if hash != expectedHash {
        renderRetargetedError(w, "refusing to apply: the dashboard data changed
        since this sync was previewed. Re-open the sync preview and try again",
        http.StatusConflict, ...)
So a user with two same-named flagged major expenses gets an intermittent 409
on apply, on data that never changed — and re-opening the preview does not
reliably fix it. This is the "preview/apply hash equality breaks" failure the
decision was written to prevent.

REACHABILITY (this is not a hypothetical invalid state): majorexpenses.Validate
(internal/services/majorexpenses/validate.go, read in full) enforces name
non-empty and <=200 chars. It does NOT enforce uniqueness. Duplicate names are
accepted by both the web form and the MCP curate path.

NEWLY INTRODUCED BY SY1, not pre-existing: `ExcludedGroups` is the only slice
in `syncPlan` sorted on a non-unique key. The pre-existing sibling sort
(incomePatterns by Description, sync.go:233) is safe because
insights.IncomePatterns groups BY description internally, so its keys are
unique by construction. The comment at sync.go:227-231 spells out this exact
hazard for that slice; SY1 added a slice that does not meet the same bar.

CORROBORATING MUTATION: removing the sort entirely (M4) does NOT fail any
test — `go test -overlay=<M4> -run TestComputeDashboardSync ./internal/handlers/whatif/`
returns `ok budget2/internal/handlers/whatif 0.034s`. The worker's
TestComputeDashboardSync_ExcludedGroupsOrderIsDeterministic uses a fixture
that yields exactly ONE excluded group, so the sort it claims to guard is
unobservable to it. The test survives deletion of the thing it guards.

WHAT IS MISSING (precise): a deterministic tiebreaker on the ExcludedGroups
ordering — e.g. sort by (Name, def.ID) with the ID carried on
syncExcludedGroup or captured in the comparator — plus a determinism test
whose fixture contains at least TWO excluded groups, so the assertion is not
vacuous. The current test cannot detect the regression it is named for.

